<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/05/marked-3-header-rb.7533.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>Public posts, profiles, and collections on <a href="https://share.markedapp.com">Marked Share</a> now get their own share card. Paste a link into Mastodon, Slack, iMessage, or a blog comment and you should see the title, byline, and date on a Marked-colored card instead of the generic site image.</p>

<p>Private documents stay private. They keep the default image and never spend a generate.</p>

<h3 id="what-the-card-looks-like">What the card looks like</h3>

<p>The card is 1200×630, paper-colored, with the Marked icon and a highlighter bar behind the title. Three lines of title, then an ellipsis. Author and date sit underneath.</p>

<p>Here&rsquo;s what that looks like when a post lands on Mastodon:</p>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/09/share-card-mastodon.jpg"></p>

<p>Drop your screenshot in as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/images/share-card-mastodon.png</code> (or rewrite the path).</p>

<p>Cards generate in the background when you publish or when the title, byline, or date changes. Body-only edits do not. The page always points at a stable URL on Marked Share (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/d/…/og.png</code>, plus matching URLs for profiles and collections), so crawlers are not chasing a temporary image host.</p>

<h3 id="snapsly-does-the-pixels">Snapsly does the pixels</h3>

<p>I generate the PNGs with <a href="https://snapsly.dev/">Snapsly</a>. One POST, a template, a PNG back. No browser farm of my own, no &ldquo;please wait while we screenshot a page&rdquo; in the publish path.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Snapsly is the easiest OG image API I have used. I send title, author, and date, and I get a 1200×630 card. That is the whole integration.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>If you need share cards and do not want to babysit Playwright, start at <a href="https://snapsly.dev/">snapsly.dev</a>.</p>

<h3 id="sharing-a-highlight">Sharing a highlight</h3>

<p>The document card is for the whole post. A highlight is a different share: one passage, as an image you can drop into Mastodon, Bluesky, or a Slack thread.</p>

<p>Click a highlight and the action bar now has a share icon. That builds a 1080×1080 PNG of the passage — document title up top, the quote in a coral-to-purple gradient, attribution underneath. Download it, or copy the image and paste it into a post.</p>

<p>The PNG is drawn in the browser. Nothing goes to Snapsly, and comment-only pins do not get a card. Anyone who can see the highlight can make the image.</p>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/09/highlight-share.jpg"></p>

<h3 id="other-recent-bits">Other recent bits</h3>

<p>While I was in there, a few other things landed:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Download and Print</strong> menus on the document preview, including print with highlights or a clean page.</li>
  <li><strong>Finish editing</strong> now includes visibility, so you can go public without a second trip through Share.</li>
  <li><strong>Welcome email</strong> after signup, and password reset by email, slug, or display name.</li>
  <li><strong>Micro.blog</strong> posts publish as timeline entries, not just a feed ping.</li>
  <li>Keyboard help for Vimium/Vimari, plus some library header and upload-tooltip cleanup.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of that is huge. All of it was in the way of actually using the site.</p>

